#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124228 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"}] :question/id #uuid "945f684f-9707-5a0a-b5d7-0753f11b4385" :question/math-academy-id "e-5266" :question/problem "Find the vertex and the axis of symmetry of the elliptic paraboloid $z= 4x^2 + 8(y + 2)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is \n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-0 & =4{x}^{2}+8(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{1/8} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{1/8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis that has its vertex at $(0,-2,0).$  Since it is oriented along the $z$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124229 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/id #uuid "7e876c7b-849f-59ce-a654-881ec9464475" :question/math-academy-id "e-5269" :question/problem "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+1=\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4} - (y-2) ^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}-(y-2)^{2} \\\\ z-(-1) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}-(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(1,2,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124283 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/id #uuid "5ce5b329-ac5d-5581-b396-eadd569b9bec" :question/math-academy-id "e-6087" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4} - \\dfrac{(y+5) ^2}{3} = z-1\n$$\n\nand the plane $x=2$  onto the $yz$-plane is a parabola. Find the vertex of this parabola." :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $x=2,$ we substitute $x=2$ into the equation of the paraboloid and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =z-1 \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =z-1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =z-1 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{3}(y+5)^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProjecting this onto the $yz$-plane, we get the equation of a parabola that has its vertex at $(-5,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d351a1ae-fbf5-526e-a74d-c5ebc4a1f3b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316243} {:answer/id #uuid "a65c3cb5-94e9-5e06-990b-ad47868d5e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316244} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0e75c4-9f3c-5053-999a-fcc40e3e6976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316245} {:answer/id #uuid "531834aa-b0b0-57f2-8669-46d4ea92eb2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316246} {:answer/id #uuid "e8dc0bc3-4d59-5a8e-b54f-5b33b8c3b820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d351a1ae-fbf5-526e-a74d-c5ebc4a1f3b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316243} :answer-field/id #uuid "019ac036-0abd-5183-852b-1353823ad195" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7859670a-81dd-5793-b67c-4dd38017890f" :question/math-academy-id "q-164265" :question/problem "Find the vertex of the elliptic paraboloid $3z+6=3(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z+6 & =3(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ 3(z+2) & =3(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z+2 & =(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z-(-2) & =(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid that has its vertex at $(3,-2,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0a10620-ca8a-5fc1-89f1-875f2b5a6904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316250} {:answer/id #uuid "812c118e-f906-53b7-ad74-af72498ef29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=3 \\\\ z=-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316251} {:answer/id #uuid "df136940-dcc7-5f3c-930f-7bfbe8ea4e89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316252} {:answer/id #uuid "41501dc7-9a78-513e-8167-fcbae528db4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=3 \\\\ z=1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316253} {:answer/id #uuid "3e9b6dca-c841-5df9-84b7-a0a8cd79bc71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=3 \\\\ y=1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "812c118e-f906-53b7-ad74-af72498ef29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=3 \\\\ z=-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316251} :answer-field/id #uuid "35258bf4-5705-5f8f-a501-65d3b320c69d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94c5e070-73bb-51c9-9483-9e3d8ed68932" :question/math-academy-id "q-164269" :question/problem "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{6}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\nx-0=\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z-(-1))^{2}}{6}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $x$-axis that has its vertex at $(0,3,-1).$\n Since it is oriented along the $x$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=3 \\\\ z=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a461af0-8f31-5b21-a841-4445fea52f9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=4 \\\\ z=-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316257} {:answer/id #uuid "a642dfe6-440b-5fe6-b7cb-450e8b855422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=4 \\\\ y=2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316258} {:answer/id #uuid "d3897975-fc80-5080-97d1-342bbac83079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-4 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316259} {:answer/id #uuid "04d49428-2cde-54c4-80a2-c31ab39391d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=-1 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316260} {:answer/id #uuid "283264f1-4bec-5cfe-862f-16bd5e416058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-4 \\\\ y=-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3897975-fc80-5080-97d1-342bbac83079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-4 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316259} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4ac7550-f87d-51a7-b575-b4396678b220" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a5bc5f3e-997e-5815-95fc-e6a4a5e517e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-164270" :question/problem "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $y+1=\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{3}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\ny-(-1)=\\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $y$-axis that has its vertex at $(-4,-1,2).$\n Since it is oriented along the $y$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2852dd76-2277-5d7f-bade-b7881d93f419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(5,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316264} {:answer/id #uuid "8c656b83-ff58-556b-838c-e4e99a3a2b5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,0,0)$ and $(2,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316265} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7906f1-f524-5662-b765-11ca37a93260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(7,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316266} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f30924-78d4-5a15-9151-ca04b9f52dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,0)$ and $(2,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316267} {:answer/id #uuid "17e2c308-4c77-5ce3-aef4-1b31b49ada7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17e2c308-4c77-5ce3-aef4-1b31b49ada7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316268} :answer-field/id #uuid "84643361-8a4b-582d-b7c7-1dea547080ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe457763-65d9-5792-9c92-9c8164b7f79d" :question/math-academy-id "q-164276" :question/problem "Given the hyperbolic paraboloid $2(x-5)^{2}-3(y-1)^{2}=z+5$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $y=0$ and $z=0$ in the equation of the paraboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-5)^{2}-3(0-1)^{2} & =0+5 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-3 & =5 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =5\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $x=3$ and $x=7.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(3,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116365e0-908f-55e4-863c-a4a8f57e20b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316271} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9e8f16-699a-59c2-803f-80f4b1b46cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316272} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9ef105-bd46-59cb-98e4-3a819bbd782c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316273} {:answer/id #uuid "3f7874b9-57de-5777-abe3-4b6997bcc4d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316274} {:answer/id #uuid "34106f8e-07d2-5126-a941-07361f990c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34106f8e-07d2-5126-a941-07361f990c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316275} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a6f92b3-41fb-58d0-b775-905658b2c610" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1dfc46d0-73fb-5732-9512-b29eeb4abe56" :question/math-academy-id "q-164282" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=z+8$ and the plane \n$z=4$  onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $z=4$, we substitute $z=4$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=z+8 \\\\ 4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=4+8 \\\\ 4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=12 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006575c1-166b-588d-a44d-089d061fcce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola: $z=\\frac{1}{27}(y-1)^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316278} {:answer/id #uuid "0aa28d17-31fd-5ce1-9bd6-ab74baa6c5d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two intersecting lines: $y=\\pm 3(x+2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316279} {:answer/id #uuid "90e98d94-3f3b-5275-910e-c7f59f99c41b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two parallel lines: $x=3(z\\pm 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316280} {:answer/id #uuid "5b79251a-8204-5b0e-b745-15da44fe53a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola: $z=27(y-1)^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316281} {:answer/id #uuid "60fced49-57fd-5317-b4d5-2477e9dda21b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $(y-1)^{2}-{z}^{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "006575c1-166b-588d-a44d-089d061fcce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola: $z=\\frac{1}{27}(y-1)^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316278} :answer-field/id #uuid "c50c9d03-bbb6-5c45-b005-5339de96dbde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69276016-ba7c-50d9-a595-a7d6a823a97c" :question/math-academy-id "q-35577" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic paraboloid $z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{27}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{{0}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{27} \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{27}(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us the equation of the parabola in $yz$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e33243f9-bc62-5ece-9da3-3ee3df191a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316285} {:answer/id #uuid "0a5e6097-2617-5ac7-b9c0-becce4d07ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316286} {:answer/id #uuid "c49d7274-e3cd-54ff-aa55-7373b7cb378e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-3)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316287} {:answer/id #uuid "86b8e1cc-02a2-58ec-946f-67e7e62c2897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-3)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316288} {:answer/id #uuid "a3396991-c8d6-521c-be6d-6ad591fabd87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,3)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c49d7274-e3cd-54ff-aa55-7373b7cb378e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-3)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316287} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ddc0cfa-36b7-5ce9-8c12-6769101aa32d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c2420eb-a369-5201-ac95-195a1c7fe310" :question/math-academy-id "q-35906" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=z+2$ and the plane \n$y=3$  onto the $xz$-plane is a parabola. What is the vertex of this parabola?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $y=3$, we substitute $y=3$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =z+2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{9} & =z+2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-1 & =z+2 \\\\ z+3 & =\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4} \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+1)^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at $(-1,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf23ab8f-9ee7-56a6-a801-dd4c72540802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316292} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea09b78-4175-54bb-a762-f055d4a78f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316293} {:answer/id #uuid "24ecf297-1a11-5eba-a344-6dc1ef68cad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316294} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1534d6-a901-5c96-94c5-103da52565a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316295} {:answer/id #uuid "abe19fdc-fb69-5928-8d4e-48a390c910a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24ecf297-1a11-5eba-a344-6dc1ef68cad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316294} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a498074-f8f5-5aed-97eb-0ca401a831f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47157beb-5e76-50cf-b21f-0f4f2d19f147" :question/math-academy-id "q-35930" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $5(x+2)^{2}+3(y+3)^{2}=z$ and the plane $z=2$  onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. Find the center of this ellipse." :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $z=2$, we substitute $z=2$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(x+2)^{2}+3(y+3)^{2} & =z \\\\ 5(x+2)^{2}+3(y+3)^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2/5}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{2/3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse in the $xy$-plane that has its center at $(-2,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fe085ad-b76e-526d-8c68-5a460e19d40d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two intersecting lines: $y=\\pm (x-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316299} {:answer/id #uuid "f501fa73-c112-5741-a991-7f431eb1ebc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola: $z=\\frac{1}{3}(y-1)^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316300} {:answer/id #uuid "96437402-c2d3-59bd-b80a-67d75eced1f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two parallel lines: $y=(z\\pm 3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316301} {:answer/id #uuid "f7082fe7-94a0-52b1-a6fd-e63919cf2b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola: ${x}^{2}-3(y-1)^{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316302} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa89ba4-33fb-559d-8f49-d803872e363d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola: $z=3(y-1)^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f501fa73-c112-5741-a991-7f431eb1ebc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola: $z=\\frac{1}{3}(y-1)^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316300} :answer-field/id #uuid "a72c180d-db2d-56c9-b1b2-0142961a5cc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "facb394a-2e83-5e9d-9e73-9cdb8f17ac06" :question/math-academy-id "q-35931" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic paraboloid $z+1=\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3} \\\\ z+1 & =\\frac{4}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3} \\\\ z+1 & =1+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3} \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{3}(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us the equation of the parabola in $yz$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4f5ddd-2cb4-5111-b56b-9238db7b147b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-3,0)$ and $(0,5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316306} {:answer/id #uuid "13c6e298-6700-593c-91eb-c60e6402f5f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-5,0)$ and $(0,3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316307} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e9a483-6685-5c10-9d8b-a27a9359bc9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3,0)$ and $(0,5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316308} {:answer/id #uuid "5158cf94-4e04-5ffb-baf2-4dea7c4a0472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316309} {:answer/id #uuid "c867c0ac-aea4-582f-abc1-9f6f416a619a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-5,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13c6e298-6700-593c-91eb-c60e6402f5f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-5,0)$ and $(0,3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316307} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ec695dc-9340-5285-b021-38f1bc98a5e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "433e52a0-e5ed-5336-8b8f-86409bc9bbf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-72191" :question/problem "Given the elliptic paraboloid $z+13=\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+13 & =\\frac{(0-5)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ 13 & =\\frac{25}{5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ 13 & =5+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =8 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $y=3,-5.$ \n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-5,0)$ and $(0,3,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a997e587-eb09-593d-823f-c1171ebccd62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316313} {:answer/id #uuid "bc664d05-5168-5bb6-b18a-2e35d92a5445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316314} {:answer/id #uuid "74f49a52-c8ba-5664-998b-e2cac8a54932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,5,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316315} {:answer/id #uuid "4efdd0e4-a3b2-5e89-816b-86e9d992177e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316316} {:answer/id #uuid "98f0a16f-af08-5dc0-816e-b96361f3dd85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc664d05-5168-5bb6-b18a-2e35d92a5445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316314} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b39f7af-a443-521b-8397-14c520113e8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ddd8f01-464e-5983-8faa-0721c715dc0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-72301" :question/problem "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+2=\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+2 & =\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-(-2) & =\\frac{(x-(-5))^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(-5,2,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49a31e4f-20c4-5e89-b08f-35b7418e1ef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-4,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316320} {:answer/id #uuid "19f9ca9c-ecf7-597d-9290-d1a78e3f1efc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-4,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316321} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4bcb98-5300-59d7-aad5-0610d3324519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,4,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316322} {:answer/id #uuid "1de0fc1d-9ae0-55c9-9a4b-eb452429efaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-4,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316323} {:answer/id #uuid "98196d9f-de3c-5dbd-a40e-a5b2c06add05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19f9ca9c-ecf7-597d-9290-d1a78e3f1efc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-4,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316321} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe9b526b-4239-5703-bf7e-7c2e962699fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42af1dcf-c0f2-5abd-91eb-380e93297f18" :question/math-academy-id "q-72303" :question/problem "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z-5=5(x-1)^{2}-3(y+4)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-5 & =5(x-1)^{2}-3(y+4)^{2} \\\\ z-5 & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{1/5}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{1/3} \\\\ z-5 & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{1/5}-\\frac{(y-(-4))^{2}}{1/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(1,-4,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baf7cec6-e88d-56c2-9c48-3903054e2001" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-7,0,0)$ and $(1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316327} {:answer/id #uuid "768b0f49-bbf1-5f50-91aa-d55d0114efff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-7,0,0)$ and $(-1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316328} {:answer/id #uuid "7c91a071-c5ec-5caf-b66a-b3e235bc6927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316329} {:answer/id #uuid "b34a5e81-b018-5583-b2e0-63c1c1cbf54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-7,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316330} {:answer/id #uuid "aad13f89-5482-553a-aa04-652f7a9f88bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baf7cec6-e88d-56c2-9c48-3903054e2001" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-7,0,0)$ and $(1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316327} :answer-field/id #uuid "a29368ce-3c2d-52d8-950e-e689c549bb19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f548b611-e9cc-556f-bad9-d30259c57cc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-72305" :question/problem "Given the hyperbolic paraboloid $(x+3)^{2}-2(y-3)^{2}=z-2$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $y=0$ and $z=0$ in the equation of the paraboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}-2(0-3)^{2} & =0-2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}-18 & =-2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-3\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $x=-7$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(-7,0,0)$ and $(1,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91629b47-a169-5a84-b346-3a6060d427cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x =-1; y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123827} {:answer/id #uuid "eb08632d-13fc-573b-bfab-c140c22df9b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y = 2; z = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123828} {:answer/id #uuid "0d180240-4603-530c-ab5d-91e22c827fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x =-1; z = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123829} {:answer/id #uuid "3e124fab-0af2-5732-8b6e-0584d5c10112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x = 1; y =-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123830} {:answer/id #uuid "76337fc6-9453-5cd5-a73c-79bfd0e0fde7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x = 1; y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91629b47-a169-5a84-b346-3a6060d427cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x =-1; y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123827} :answer-field/id #uuid "e08caaef-c397-5aa6-9fa9-db3cd4c262e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef1d67f8-50b7-560a-9d0b-39e4e445375c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73856" :question/problem "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $z=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\nz-0=\\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis that has its vertex at $(-1,2,0).$\n Since it is oriented along the $z$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1 \\\\ y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95c59a14-cc79-59b4-8ecf-e486e4f7018d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316334} {:answer/id #uuid "0c0d047d-a606-5618-8f53-11c0a6efbe61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316335} {:answer/id #uuid "bd14b152-c67b-5c85-b78f-a43d9ff7cb90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316336} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5afda9-c464-5a2e-90e4-ee5e710ecc58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-4,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316337} {:answer/id #uuid "96b87072-672c-5255-88d2-98fe3bac6e0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c0d047d-a606-5618-8f53-11c0a6efbe61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316335} :answer-field/id #uuid "72d0034c-8f66-508b-a1ca-d24d9c93dfcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a799b397-3baf-556b-b3f7-a1b675616884" :question/math-academy-id "q-73862" :question/problem "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z-3=\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-3 & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4} \\\\ z-3 & =\\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(-2,4,3).$"}]}
